Play-to-earn gets back in the game

If you've ever played =E2=80=9CAngry Birds=E2=80=9D while waiting for the b=
us and thought, "I should be getting paid for this," boy, do we have news f=
or you.

MoneyTime (https://www.moneytime.dev/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_ZPIp2BXops7uDYryeAB=
rxACXZgIS7LrY4xBB8Hw9R6bvoim89olwHNBU7D7fK96nO3Tk795uagLdMQMotftrTCwtWGg&_h=
smi=3D400745480 ) , creator of a mobile app that "turns play time into pay =
time," recently closed $3m (https://nextbiggames.com/2026/01/20/%F0%9F%92%B=
0-moneytime-raises-3m-seed-to-reignite-play-to-earn-on-mobile/?_hsenc=3Dp2A=
Nqtz-_ZPIp2BXops7uDYryeABrxACXZgIS7LrY4xBB8Hw9R6bvoim89olwHNBU7D7fK96nO3Tk7=
95uagLdMQMotftrTCwtWGg&_hsmi=3D400745480 ) in seed funding. It's a play-to-=
earn platform =E2=80=94 a term often associated with a controversial busine=
ss model that peaked in the early 2020s.

What went wrong?

=E2=80=9CAxie Infinity,=E2=80=9D a Pok=C3=A9mon-esque game where players ba=
ttled cartoon creatures, launched in 2018 =E2=80=94 probably the most promi=
nent play-to-earn app.

- Players paid hundreds of dollars to access the game and compete for brand=
-specific cryptocurrency.

- It spawned an exploitative micro-industry in the Philippines, promising c=
ompetitive income in the country's pandemic-ravaged economy.

- Investors fronted access fees for players in exchange for as much as 50% =
(https://time.com/6199385/axie-infinity-crypto-game-philippines-debt/?_hsen=
c=3Dp2ANqtz-_ZPIp2BXops7uDYryeABrxACXZgIS7LrY4xBB8Hw9R6bvoim89olwHNBU7D7fK9=
6nO3Tk795uagLdMQMotftrTCwtWGg&_hsmi=3D400745480 ) of their earnings.

Several factors, including the model's over-reliance on players' continued =
investment and a $620m hack (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/cryptocu=
rrency/620-million-in-crypto-stolen-from-axie-infinitys-ronin-bridge/?_hsen=
c=3Dp2ANqtz-_ZPIp2BXops7uDYryeABrxACXZgIS7LrY4xBB8Hw9R6bvoim89olwHNBU7D7fK9=
6nO3Tk795uagLdMQMotftrTCwtWGg&_hsmi=3D400745480 ) , ultimately crashed Axie=
's tokens over 99%.

What's different now?

MoneyTime doesn't involve cryptocurrency, player buy-in, or quasi-gambling.=
 Dedicated users make $2-$5 per hour at most. It's billed as a paid hobby a=
s opposed to a meaningful income source.

Developers pay to have their games pushed to the platform's users =E2=80=94=
 and a portion of MoneyTime's revenue is distributed to users who play thos=
e games on the app. A few other key players are in the space:

- Mistplay (https://www.mistplay.com/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_ZPIp2BXops7uDYryeAB=
rxACXZgIS7LrY4xBB8Hw9R6bvoim89olwHNBU7D7fK96nO3Tk795uagLdMQMotftrTCwtWGg&_h=
smi=3D400745480 ) surpassed 10m downloads, offering gift cards in exchange =
for game time.

- Swagbucks (https://www.swagbucks.com/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_ZPIp2BXops7uDYrye=
ABrxACXZgIS7LrY4xBB8Hw9R6bvoim89olwHNBU7D7fK96nO3Tk795uagLdMQMotftrTCwtWGg&=
_hsmi=3D400745480 ) gives cash rewards for activities beyond gaming, such a=
s taking surveys and watching videos.

- JustPlay (https://www.justplayengine.com/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_ZPIp2BXops7uD=
YryeABrxACXZgIS7LrY4xBB8Hw9R6bvoim89olwHNBU7D7fK96nO3Tk795uagLdMQMotftrTCwt=
WGg&_hsmi=3D400745480 ) lets you cash out or donate the money you earn from=
 playing games.

Of course, any tech promising passive income through a mobile app raises co=
ncerns about data privacy and potential scams. And people familiar with sto=
ries like Axie Infinity's might have reservations about anything labeled "p=
lay-to-earn."

But MoneyTime seems pretty frank about its intentions. It offers developers=
 exposure and gives users a couple of extra bucks here and there =E2=80=94 =
a tough mission for people to exploit.

